ACC-007 — Engineering Understanding

Document ID: ACC-007
Title: Engineering Understanding
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines Engineering Understanding, the central cognitive concept of the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

Engineering Understanding represents Atlas's current, coherent, and evolving representation of Engineering Reality, constructed from Engineering Evidence within a defined engineering context.

It serves as the primary cognitive artifact upon which reasoning, decision support, memory, and knowledge are built.

2. Scope

This specification applies to all Engineering Understanding maintained, evolved, or consumed within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework.

Engineering Understanding may represent:

Engineering systems
Physical assets
Digital assets
Structures
Components
Processes
Projects
Behaviors
Conditions
Risks
Constraints
Engineering problems
Engineering solutions
3. Canonical Definition

Engineering Understanding is the continuously evolving cognitive representation of Engineering Reality, constructed from Engineering Evidence, interpreted within context, and maintained to support engineering reasoning, decision-making, and knowledge evolution.

Engineering Understanding is Atlas's model of reality, not reality itself.

4. Characteristics

Engineering Understanding SHALL possess the following characteristics.

4.1 Reality-Based

Engineering Understanding SHALL represent Engineering Reality.

4.2 Evidence-Based

Engineering Understanding SHALL be supported by Engineering Evidence.

4.3 Context-Aware

Engineering Understanding SHALL always exist within an explicit engineering context.

4.4 Explainable

Every element of Engineering Understanding SHALL be explainable through supporting evidence and reasoning.

4.5 Traceable

Engineering Understanding SHALL maintain traceability to the Engineering Evidence from which it is constructed.

4.6 Evolvable

Engineering Understanding SHALL evolve as Engineering Evidence changes.

4.7 Consistent

Engineering Understanding SHALL maintain internal logical consistency.

4.8 Action-Oriented

Engineering Understanding SHALL support engineering reasoning and informed decision-making.

